---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this site stores on your device and what it never sends anywhere."
>
  <div class="container prose-page">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>Short version: nothing you do here leaves your device.</p>

    <h2>What is stored, and where</h2>
    <p>
      A few small things are kept in your browser's local storage so the site remembers you: your reading
      level, your theme (light or dark), which topics you have opened or marked complete, and, if you answer
      practice questions, your answers, the site's estimate of what you know, when each question is due for
      review and the days you practised. They live only in this browser on this device. Clearing site data
      removes them, the progress menu has a "Reset progress" button for topics, and the{' '}
      <a href="/progress/">progress page</a> can export, import or erase everything.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your browser blocks storage, the site still works. It simply forgets your choices when you close the
      page.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not done</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No analytics, no advertising and no tracking scripts.</li>
      <li>No accounts unless you choose the optional sync, and no cookies set by this site.</li>
      <li>Files you open in the tools are read in your browser and never uploaded.</li>
      <li>Search runs on an index shipped with the site, in your browser.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Optional sync</h2>
    <p>
      Some builds of the site offer an optional way to keep your practice progress across devices. It is off
      by default and nothing is sent until you press "Sign in to sync" on the{' '}
      <a href="/progress/">progress page</a>. The code that talks to the sync service is not even downloaded
      before that.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>What is stored:</strong> one private document per account holding your practice answers and
        the site's estimates of what you know (the same data as the export file), the time it was last saved,
        and the account id that Google sign-in provides. Topic progress, your practice run and your settings
        stay on each device.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Where:</strong> Google's Firebase (Cloud Firestore). Access rules allow only you, signed in,
        to read, change or delete that document.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>How often:</strong> at most about once a minute while you practise, and when you leave the
        page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>How to delete it:</strong> the sync section of the progress page has a "Delete my synced data"
        button. It removes the cloud copy and signs you out; the copy on this device is not changed. Erasing
        your progress on this device does not delete the cloud copy, so that a mistake on one device cannot
        wipe your other devices.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The pages are served by Firebase Hosting. Like any web host it keeps ordinary server logs of requests,
      which are outside this site's control.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
